keel-web (0.2.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The default site of a simple installation is keel-web's,
    /etc/nginx/sites-available/keel-default, in Debian's place (handbook
    decision 0042, the first site of Keel-Linux/tracker#51): the default
    server on 80 and 443, IPv6 first and IPv4 on, HTTP/2, TLS 1.2 and 1.3
    with the machine's certificate as turnkey-make-ssl-cert --default
    writes it (/etc/ssl/private/cert.pem, cert.key and dhparams.pem:
    keel-host-keys at the first boot, the confconsole Certificate screen
    through ACME later, in place), the ciphers of common's
    zz-ssl-ciphers, no session tickets, and no HSTS, which comes after
    ACME (tracker#51). HTTP
    is served, not redirected: ACME's http-01 needs port 80 to answer.
    /keel-health answers 204 on both schemes. On a Keel Web image, Debian's
    default site served an empty /var/www/html (nginx's bare 404) and
    nothing listened on 443.
  * The pages: a placeholder saying this is a Keel Web node with no site
    configured yet and where the operator configures one (confconsole,
    /etc/keel/instance.yaml), the site's own 404 and 50x pages,
    server_tokens off; static, no external resource, no server version.
    Installed under /usr/share/keel-web/default-site, as Debian policy
    keeps a package out of /var/www; the postinst links
    /var/www/keel-default to it, and leaves a directory of the operator's
    there alone.
  * The postinst enables the site: it removes the link of Debian's default
    site when that is the unedited one nginx-common shipped (recorded in
    /var/lib/keel-web, as the state hook did), links keel-default and
    tests with nginx -t; while the machine's certificate is not made yet,
    as in fab's chroot, the site stays enabled and nginx -t is left to the
    first boot, said in one line. An edited default site, a link of the
    operator's or none at all leaves the default server the operator's,
    and the script says so. An upgrade never re-enables a site the operator
    disabled. The postrm takes the sites out of sites-enabled on remove as
    on purge, since the pages leave with the package, and gives Debian's
    link back.
  * The anubis state hook puts the front in keel-default's place, not
    Debian's, and keel-default back when the overlay is turned off; the
    two sites are never enabled together, and Debian's default site is the
    postinst's and postrm's alone. A machine upgraded from 0.1.1 in a cloud
    mode gets keel-default the next time the overlay is turned off.
  * The front serves the same pages, /var/www/keel-default, with the 404
    and 50x pages (on 443 too, for a 502 while Anubis is down) and
    server_tokens off in each of its three servers.
  * Nginx needs the certificate to start on any port, so
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/nginx.service.d/keel-web.conf has it want and
    start after keel-host-keys.service, which makes a missing pair.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sat, 03 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0000

keel-web (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The Anubis front serves HTTPS. Anubis sets its cookies Secure, and
    the front served plain HTTP only, so no browser passed the challenge
    by name. Port 443 is the default server with TLS 1.2 and 1.3 and the
    machine's certificate (/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/cert.crt and
    /etc/ssl/private/cert.key, the default of decision 0042: self-signed
    by keel-host-keys at the first boot until the confconsole Certificate
    screen issues one through ACME), HTTP/2 on; port 80 answers 301 to
    the same URL on HTTPS. No HSTS, which over a self-signed certificate
    locks visitors out. The state hook refuses, changing nothing, while
    the certificate or its key is missing.
  * Activate nginx-reload, so an install, upgrade or remove reaches a
    running Nginx: an upgrade from 0.1.0 serves the HTTPS front at once.
  * TLS 1.2 ciphers are common's zz-ssl-ciphers list (Mozilla
    intermediate, forward secret only), ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off.
    The front notes HSTS once ACME has issued a CA's certificate, and the
    listen lines a tls-passthrough site must take away, as TODOs of
    Keel-Linux/tracker#51 (the implementation of decision 0042).
  * Depends on anubis (>= 1.27.0-0+keel3), whose pass-challenge, with
    REDIRECT_DOMAINS empty as keel-web leaves it, redirects a solved
    challenge only to the request's own origin: upstream redirected to
    any host. A list of the machine's names would break a new DNS name,
    a 0042 site or a public IP behind NAT.
  * Depends on keel-overlay-coraza (>= 0.1.2): the WAF let Anubis's
    pass-challenge through only by name, never by IP (CRS 931100 read the
    IP in its redir argument), and its audit log names the rule that
    blocked.
  * The same anubis (keel2 and on) serves the challenge to a client that
    does not accept gzip instead of answering 500 to one such request in
    64 at random (closes #2).

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

keel-web (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release (handbook decision 0041, first implementation, step 7).
    Ships the appliance manifest of Keel Web from docs/manifest-v1.md as
    /usr/share/keel/appliances/web.yaml: Core plus nginx, enabled in every
    mode, and coraza and anubis, disabled in simple and enabled in the
    cloud modes. Web has no process of its own.
  * Ships /etc/nginx/sites-available/default-anubis, Debian's default site
    behind Anubis, and /usr/lib/keel/overlays/anubis/state.d/50keel-web,
    which keel spec apply runs when the anubis overlay is turned on or
    off: it puts that front in place of Debian's default site, and back,
    tested with nginx -t and reloaded. It only ever replaces Debian's
    unedited default link: an edited default site is refused, and with no
    Debian link the operator's default server is left alone, and the hook
    says so. The change is recorded before Debian's link goes.
  * Depends on keel-core, the three Web overlays and keel (>= 0.15.0), the
    first keel that runs the overlays' state hooks, and on
    keel-overlay-coraza (>= 0.1.1), which declares its state hook and keeps
    gzip answers whole while Coraza is on
    (Keel-Linux/libnginx-mod-http-coraza#2).

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000
